omment.scan(pattern) }.flatten\n\n# Convert all elements to integer (as they will be strings after the regex)\nuser_ids = user_ids.map(&:to_i)\n```\n\nThis will give you an array of integers like `[4154, 64, 4154, 12]`. \n\nKeep in mind this is a simple example and might not cover more complex use cases. For example, it assumes that the \"@\" character is always immediately followed by the user ID without any space. It also assumes that every string of numbers is a user ID, even if it isn't preceded by the \"@\" character. You would need a more complex regex if your actual use case doesn't meet these assumptions.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1685564084.129139,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa24704-ad60-4619-9a8a-cc1cfb03e59c","source_parent_id":"6a6a0107-eb85-4b59-a9e5-76d24571a7c0","source_record_index":2,"text":"Let's say I have a table called `responses` with a text field called `comment` that can contain strings like these:\n\n\"I got a lot of help from @4154 and @64 this week.\"\n\"@4154 thanks a million!!! Also @12\"\n\nHow would I do a query using Ruby on Rails to return all of the numbers that exist in the table into an array? For example `[4154, 64, 4154, 12]`","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction":{"context":[{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["I have a table called `responses` with a text field called `comment`"],"item_id":"context_1","normalized_text":"A table named `responses` has a text field named `comment`.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]},{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["\"I got a lot of help from @4154 and @64 this week.\"","\"@4154 thanks a million!!! Also @12\""],"item_id":"context_2","normalized_text":"Example comments contain numbers prefixed with `@`, such as `@4154`, `@64`, and `@12`.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]},{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["How would I do a query using Ruby on Rails"],"item_id":"cont